Original commit body from 7f268ddcb24083ba81a9a482e3fa4b7dca3262e9:

`scripts/release-check` runs under `set -euo pipefail`, and wrote:

    AUDIT_OUTPUT=$(pip-audit ... )
    AUDIT_EXIT=$?
    if [ $AUDIT_EXIT -eq 0 ]; then pass; else echo "$AUDIT_OUTPUT"; fail; fi

Under `set -e` a simple assignment whose command substitution exits non-zero
terminates the script AT THE ASSIGNMENT. `AUDIT_EXIT=$?` never ran, the if/else
below it was unreachable, and the `echo "$AUDIT_OUTPUT"` that prints the
findings could not execute. So could nothing after it: secrets scan, code
quality, test suite, build verification, tag state -- all skipped.

The script's own comment described the opposite intent: "pip-audit's exit code
is the gate: 0 = no vulnerabilities, 1 = found. Anything else (network, parse,
unsupported package) is a real failure we want to surface, not paper over."

BEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE, before and after, on the real script:

  before  31 lines, ending mid-section at "  Running pip-audit...", exit 1.
          No findings, no verdict. Sections reached: 1, 2, 2b, 3(partial).
  after   114 lines, the six findings printed in full, and sections 4 (Code
          Quality) and 5 (Test Suite) REACHED for the first time.

THE FIX IS `|| AUDIT_EXIT=$?` WITH `AUDIT_EXIT=0` INITIALIZED. The initializer
is not decoration: on a CLEAN audit the `||` branch never runs, so without it
`set -u` fails -- and only on the passing path, which is not the case anyone is
thinking about when they edit this block. The test harness caught exactly that
during development, which is why the block extraction is anchored on the
initializer rather than on the pip-audit call.

STRUCTURAL SWEEP, because a bug in one script is a question about the rest:
every other exit-code capture in scripts/ and .githooks/ already uses the safe
`cmd || var=$?` idiom, or sits in an `if` condition where set -e does not apply
(auto-pr:1765). release-check:251 was the only instance. Not a class.

THE TESTS EXECUTE THE SHIPPED BLOCK rather than grepping it, because a guard
that cannot be SHOWN to fire is indistinguishable from one matching nothing.
The block is extracted from the real script, run under real `set -euo pipefail`
against a stub pip-audit at exits 1/2/127, and asserted to print the findings
AND let a later marker run. The load-bearing test is the DIFFERENTIAL: it
reconstructs the pre-fix form and proves it behaves differently. Without an
input that separates the two rules, "the fixed block reports findings" would be
satisfiable for reasons having nothing to do with the fix.

TWO CONSEQUENCES OF A GATE THAT NOW REPORTS, both handled here rather than left
for the release to trip over:

1. Six findings became visible: cryptography 48.0.1 (PYSEC-2026-3552/3553/3554)
   and nltk 3.9.4 (PYSEC-2026-3582/3583/3584). All have fix versions, so they
   are PINNED, not ignored -- which is the script's own stated policy ("fixable
   deps are pinned above, not ignored here"). pyopenssl>=26.4.0 joins them
   because cryptography 50 cannot resolve against pyOpenSSL 26.0.0, and 26.4.0
   is what CI already installs. NEITHER PACKAGE IS A SHIPPED DEPENDENCY --
   verified via importlib.metadata, not assumed: cryptography arrives through
   Authlib/py-webauthn and nltk through nothing. No released artifact was ever
   affected.
2. A DEAD IGNORE was removed and the surviving one re-verified, answering half
   of WI-totak with evidence instead of a re-read. PYSEC-2026-597 was carried as
   "nltk, no fix available"; nltk 3.10 fixes it, and dropping the ignore leaves
   the audit clean. A dead ignore in a security gate is worse than the advisory
   it names: it is a false statement about what was checked, and it suppresses
   the NEXT advisory for that package. The diskcache carve-out was tested the
   same way and stays -- dropping it surfaces PYSEC-2026-2447, the same advisory
   under its PYSEC id, still with no fix version.

Section 3 now reports "✓ No known vulnerabilities in dependencies" on a full
run. 6 passed in the new suite.

